  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<ul><li>Reaches almost 10 cm in length.</li><li>Keep in a group of 6 or more.</li><li>Temperature 18–28°C.</li><li>Peaceful towards all tank mates.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>The emerald catfish is imported regularly from the upper Amazon drainage in Peru. The type specimen of Callichthys splendens (today Brochis splendens), described by Castelnau in 1855, came from the Rio Tocantins in Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>In terms of care, Brochis splendens does not differ significantly from most Corydoras species. Keep these fish in a group of at least 6. The tank should not be too small, as B. splendens can reach almost 10 cm. The bottom should consist of fine, soft sand at least in places.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>Any tap water suitable as drinking water is suitable for keeping them. The water temperature can be between 18 and 28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>Every usual ornamental fish food is eaten.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>Completely peaceful towards all tank mates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-splendens-peru-2/",
      "html": "<p>From the Peru region, no fewer than four synonyms of this armored catfish, spread over three genera (Brochis, Chaenothorax and Corydoras), were described by Edward Drinker Cope (1840–1897). This shows how different the fish can look. It is agreed that several species hide behind the name Brochis splendens, but no comprehensive revision currently exists.</p><p>In a tank of Peru imports, animals with small dots in the dorsal fin were noticed; this fin is usually completely transparent in B. splendens. At the same time one male developed, possibly because of unusually high water temperatures during a heat wave, a courtship dress and distinctly long extended pectoral fin spines. The courtship dress is a darkening of the body and a brightening of the head area, the two zones sharply separated by a bright vertical line. The matching female and the other animals kept the usual shiny emerald green coloration.</p>"
    }
  ],
